        <h2>Summary / Description</h2>
        <div class="description">
          <B>Objectives:</B><P>Several lines of evidence suggest that there may be compromises of immune responses in humans or experimental animals exposed to weightlessness conditions during space flight, or maintained in hypodynamic, hypokinetic, antiorthostatic models simulating some aspects of weightlessness.  Among those possible immune responses and is the interferon (IFN) system.  The present study was carried out to determine the effects of a period of weightlessness on IFN production by rats and to determine if the animal system could be useful in evaluating the effects of space flight on IFN.<P><B>Approach:</B><P>Spleens were removed from rats, separated into individual cells, suspended in RPMI-1640 medium, and supplemented with antibiotics, L-glutamine, 2-mercaptoethanol, and 10% fetal bovine serum.  Cultures were then stimulated with concanavalin A (either 1mg/ml or 0.1mg/ml)and incubated at 37 degrees C for 48 hours to induce IFN-gamma.  Supernatant fluids were harvested from the culture and frozen, resulting in production of IFN-gamma in the fluids.  These were next analyzed for IFN antiviral activity.  Due to cross reaction of rat and mouse IFN, the assay was carried out on mouse L-929 cells using the Indiana strain of vesicular somatitis virus as the target.  Assays used were either a plaque reduction or microplaque reduction, and the IFN titer corresponded to the reciprocal of the greatest dilution of the test sample that reduced virus plaques by 50%.<P><B>Results:</B><P>Spleens of all flown rats were substantially reduced in weight compared to controls.  Spleen cells from rats flown in the SL-3 mission, thereby exposed to weightlessness, had very much reduced production of IFN-gamma when compared to controls.  Spleen cells from 7 of the 10 control rats produced IFN-gamma.  In contrast, only 1 of 10 flown rats produced minimal levels  and the other flown rats produced no detectable levels of IFN-gamma.<br><br>The reason for this result could be due to the decreased size of the spleen in flown rats.  It is possible that T-lymphocytes that reside in the spleen and are responsible for IFN-gamma production could have been depleted as a result of the flight.  The drop in IFN production could also have been related to the effects of the weightlessness condition, or in part  to the stress on handling during the flight.  There has been no determination as to the permanent or transient nature of the drop in IFN-gamma production; i.e. would production have returned to normal had the rats been allowed to recover on the ground for a period of time before sacrifice.  This would require further experiments.
        </div>

